# Data source: "demo" (localStorage DemoStore, default) or "api" (real REST backend)
VITE_DATA_SOURCE=demo

# Base URL of the /api/v1 REST backend. Used only when VITE_DATA_SOURCE=api.
VITE_API_BASE_URL=/api/v1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secret access path (private deployment)
#
# When set, the panel renders ONLY at https://<host>/<VITE_ACCESS_PATH>/ (or
# ?k=<value>). Any other URL returns a bare "404 Not Found" body with no
# branding, so scanners record nothing.
#
# Generate one with:   npm run gen:access-path
#
# This is OBSCURITY, NOT SECURITY. A URL leaks through history, Referer
# headers, bookmark sync and screenshots. It removes opportunistic scanners; it
# is never a substitute for authentication. Leave empty to disable the gate.
#
# Must be 24-128 chars of [A-Za-z0-9_-]; a shorter value is rejected at startup.
VITE_ACCESS_PATH=

# Optional: pin the API to an absolute origin. Leave as a relative path so the
# browser never talks to a different host than the one serving the panel.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local API development (dev-server proxy)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# With the default relative base above, the browser requests /api/v1/... from
# whatever origin serves the panel. Production satisfies that at the edge
# (deploy/nginx.conf routes /api to the backend). The Vite DEV SERVER does the
# same job through a dev-only proxy in vite.config.ts:
#
#     /api  →  http://127.0.0.1:8080
#
# So `npm run dev` with VITE_DATA_SOURCE=api talks to a backend on port 8080 —
# start it there with `php artisan serve --port=8080` inside `backend/` — with
# no CORS configuration and no absolute URL in the source. Path and query are
# forwarded unchanged (/api/v1/students?page=2 arrives intact).
#
# The proxy exists only in the dev server: it is not part of the build, so it
# never appears in dist/ and adds no production behaviour. There is deliberately
# no `preview` proxy — `npm run preview` serves the built artifact as-is, and
# forwarding its /api calls would disguise an unconfigured deployment as a
# working one.
